{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Shimon and Levi, etc.</b> He says <b>brothers</b> because there exists between them a brotherhood in their nature and a single disposition equal to the disposition of brothers, and this quality is not equal in all brothers. Or it may intend to give a reason for their deeds which they did in the matter of Shechem and in the matter of Joseph, saying that their nature is mighty in the predominance of the element of fire. And you will find that they said, their memory for blessing (Yoma 22b): Saul committed one offense and the kingship was taken from him, and David committed many and it was not taken from him. And the reason is because Saul's nature was balanced, while David's nature was hot, as it says (1 Samuel 16:12): ״וְהוּא אַדְמוֹנִי״. And HaShem judges a man according to his composition, for one in whom the element of fire predominates, even though he may exert himself with every ounce of zeal in the service of HaShem, will not escape error. And with a small measure of the self-exertion that a balanced person undertakes, he will save himself from errors. And He says that Shimon and Levi are <b>brothers</b> — denoting heat, and therefore He cursed their anger and their wrath because it was fierce, and it is well known that anger and wrath grow great from the element of fire when it increases." "And He says that because they are in the predominance of heat, therefore <b>instruments of violence are their kin</b>. And He compared their deeds to instruments of violence, to say that they did not inherit their disposition from the root of the soul of their progenitor, but rather instruments of violence are things that are recognized as belonging to them. And the meaning of <b>their kin</b> is in the sense of recognition — that their father produced nothing in them but the predominance of fire, but they, by cultivating this matter, committed deeds that were not proper, as in the matter of Joseph." "<b>Further,</b> He may intend in saying <b>instruments of violence</b> etc., that the sale with which they sold Joseph — they sold him as violence, for that which he was not liable to them in judgment. And although all the brothers sold him, when you consider carefully you will see that they were the principals: at first, when he arrived, they said each man to his brother, etc., and they said, their memory for blessing (Genesis Rabbah 99:6), that they were Shimon and Levi, and they were the ones who counseled to kill him, and everything came from them, and so it is in the words of our Rabbis, their memory for blessing."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:24.774979507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}